reason:
I stay at v1 (block_k 3, lambda 0.5) and follow the LLM's recommendation.

Option 1 is not an acceptable remedy for C. It passes the vote 2-1 (A ×, B ○, C ○), but it worsens every KPI measure read under K1, and customers would pay for C's gain:
- on-time falls from 213.6 to 204.0 (-9.6, 0/5 seeds improved);
- late rises from 9.2 to 18.8;
- p95_wait_s rises from 130 to 182, and max_wait_s from 236 to 334;
- the lowest on-time rate falls from 0.93 to 0.83;
- road violations nearly double, from 24.2 to 44.6, and blocked seconds rise from 1888 to 3085.
Removing enforcement entirely trades road order and customer service for one company's utility (C 58.3 to 70.2). That is not the targeted remedy that D1 and D2 asked for. Departing from the vote result is deliberate: a passing vote does not oblige adoption when the KPI clearly worsens for customers, who do not vote.

Option 2 is the same block_k change that lost 1-2 in D1 and D2, and it lost 1-2 again (A ○, B ×, C ×). The conditions set in D1 and D2 are still unmet:
- there is no remedy for C, whose utility would fall from 58.3 to 46.9 (-11.4), the largest drop so far, from the lowest-utility company;
- there is no per-company, per-type or per-severity breakdown of the added road violations (24.2 to 34.8).
The evidence is also weaker than before: +5.8 with only 3/5 seeds improved, versus 5/5 in D1 and D2. A first override of a two-of-three majority on weaker evidence, with the stated conditions unmet, would make those conditions meaningless and damage trust in the vote.

I accept the cost to customers of waiting another period: about 5.8 fewer on-time deliveries per run and a p95 wait of 130 s instead of 93.2 s.

The next proposal must combine block_k=2 with a partial or redistributed sanction change, measured together and with a violation breakdown. D1.f1, D1.f2, D2.f1 and D2.f2 are not fulfilled: lambda 0 was proposed separately from block_k=2, was not paired with it in a vote, and no breakdown was provided.

I stay at v1 (block_k 3, hold_limit 60s) and follow the LLM's recommendation.

Option 1 (hold_limit 60s → 70s) is not a remedy, and a sequenced package does not meet my D3 condition. D3 asked for block_k=2 and a sanction change to be measured and voted on together. A first leg adopted now binds nothing about the second leg. Customers would bear its cost even if block_k=2 then fails its vote again. The measured cost is real and nobody gains:
- on-time falls from 264.6 to 260.6 (-4.0, 1/5 seeds improved);
- late rises from 10.0 to 14.0;
- p95_wait_s rises from 121 to 141, and max_wait_s from 284 to 292;
- the lowest on-time rate falls from 0.93 to 0.89;
- blocked s rises from 1855 to 2042.
Utility also falls for C (77.7 to 77.2) and B (80.3 to 79.9), the companies it was meant to relieve, as well as for A (92.2 to 88.6). The only improvement is road violations, which fall from 20.4 to 19.0.
Departing from the vote result (A ×, B ○, C ○) is deliberate. A passing vote does not oblige adoption when every customer measure read under K1 worsens, and customers do not vote.

Option 2 (block_k 3 → 2) is the same change I declined in D1 to D3, and it lost 1-2 again (A ○, B ×, C ×). The evidence is strong: on-time rises from 264.6 to 272.6 (+8.0, 5/5 seeds), late falls from 10.0 to 2.0, and p95_wait_s falls from 121 to 90.0. But the conditions I set are still unmet:
- there is no remedy for C, whose utility would fall from 77.7 to 68.8 and which is already the lowest-utility company;
- there is no per-company breakdown of the added road violations (20.4 to 29.6).
A first override of a two-of-three majority with these conditions unmet would make them meaningless.

I accept the customer cost of waiting: about 8 fewer on-time deliveries per run and a p95 wait of 121 s instead of 90.0 s.

My reading of the regulation: the one-parameter, one-step format cannot satisfy the joint-measurement condition by sequencing. A procedure for measuring a combined package is needed, for example a measurement-only joint run outside the amendment format, or a regulatory change that the regulation owner must decide.

No open follow-up is fulfilled. No block_k=2 plus sanction package was measured together, and no breakdown was provided.

I adopt sanction_lambda 0.5 → 1, following the LLM's recommendation and the vote (A ○, B ○, C ×). The C-remedy condition I set in D1 to D4 was a condition for overriding a two-of-three majority on block_k=2. It was never a general veto for C on any enforcement change. Here no vote is overridden. In D3 and D4 I rejected passing changes because customers, who do not vote, would pay for them. Here customers are the main beneficiaries on every measure read under K1:
- on-time rises from 74.8 to 82.6 (+7.8, 5/5 seeds improved);
- late falls from 7.8 to 0.0;
- p95_wait_s falls from 269 to 71.4, and max_wait_s from 533 to 102;
- unfinished stays at 0.0;
- the lowest on-time rate rises from 0.83 to 1.00.
Road order also improves: blocked s falls from 2396 to 400 and road violations from 20.6 to 18.2. So the unanalysed-violation concern from D1 and D2 does not arise.
The cost to C is real. Its utility falls from 15.9 to 10.0, and it is already the lowest-utility company. The cost comes from sanctions on overstays that block A and B, so it falls on the behaviour that harms others. Rejecting the change would keep customers at 7.8 late deliveries per run and a 533 s worst-case wait to protect that behaviour. I do not treat C's loss as acceptable without attention: I require a C-specific analysis and a targeted remedy proposal next period, plus a check that the gains hold. This change raises lambda, the opposite direction of the D3 idea of a lower lambda paired with block_k=2. Any future block_k=2 package must be measured against the new baseline. No open follow-up is fulfilled: no joint package procedure, violation breakdown or vote-reason inquiry was provided.
